WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.200
When a notification appears on an iOS screen,

2
00:00:03.200 --> 00:00:08.440
the operating system has already processed a specific sequence of logic gates in the background.

3
00:00:08.440 --> 00:00:13.000
Within the Expo ecosystem, Expo notifications manages these events.

4
00:00:13.000 --> 00:00:17.440
However, Expo does not use a proprietary push engine to achieve this.

5
00:00:17.440 --> 00:00:22.040
The underlying framework that dictates every notification behavior on an Apple device

6
00:00:22.040 --> 00:00:25.240
is the native iOS UN notification center.

7
00:00:25.240 --> 00:00:28.240
Expo acts as a transparent JavaScript wrapper,

8
00:00:28.240 --> 00:00:30.960
providing a direct bridge to this native system.

9
00:00:30.960 --> 00:00:33.680
This wrapper unifies three key areas,

10
00:00:33.680 --> 00:00:35.680
OS-level permission requests,

11
00:00:35.680 --> 00:00:37.440
local scheduling on the device,

12
00:00:37.440 --> 00:00:41.000
and remote delivery via the Apple Push notification service.

13
00:00:41.000 --> 00:00:46.680
This architecture means developers can interact with these systems without writing swift or objective C code.

14
00:00:46.680 --> 00:00:49.640
By understanding how iOS handles these primitives,

15
00:00:49.640 --> 00:00:54.640
you can ensure the JavaScript thread produces the exact native behavior your application requires.

16
00:00:54.800 --> 00:00:58.400
The first requirement of the iOS model is a shared permission state.

17
00:00:58.400 --> 00:01:03.480
Apple does not provide separate prompts for local works and remote push notifications.

18
00:01:03.480 --> 00:01:09.440
This is governed by the native function UN notification center.request authorization.

19
00:01:09.440 --> 00:01:15.200
In this diagram, we can see that both local and remote data paths are blocked by this single gate.

20
00:01:15.200 --> 00:01:20.000
iOS architecture requires that authorization be explicitly requested and granted

21
00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:23.240
before any notification can be scheduled or received.

22
00:01:23.320 --> 00:01:27.160
This system dialogue will only appear once per application install.

23
00:01:27.160 --> 00:01:29.160
If a user denies this initial prompt,

24
00:01:29.160 --> 00:01:33.080
the operating system registers a permanent denied status for the app.

25
00:01:33.080 --> 00:01:38.680
All subsequent JavaScript permission requests will return denied immediately without showing a dialogue.

26
00:01:38.680 --> 00:01:43.400
This requires developers to implement custom UI to guide users into the iOS settings app

27
00:01:43.400 --> 00:01:45.960
to manually re-enable notifications.

28
00:01:45.960 --> 00:01:49.640
Because this one action dictates the entire notification lifecycle,

29
00:01:49.640 --> 00:01:53.720
managing the permission state is the necessary first step of the system architecture.

30
00:01:53.720 --> 00:01:56.600
Local notifications operate differently.

31
00:01:56.600 --> 00:02:02.040
They require no server interaction and do not communicate with the Apple Push notification service.

32
00:02:02.760 --> 00:02:06.120
These events are mapped to UN notification trigger

33
00:02:06.120 --> 00:02:09.480
and processed entirely within the device's internal environment.

34
00:02:10.200 --> 00:02:13.720
The API provides two main triggers for these events,

35
00:02:13.720 --> 00:02:18.200
a time interval for delays and a calendar trigger for specific dates and times.

36
00:02:18.840 --> 00:02:22.920
This is the same underlying API used by native iOS applications,

37
00:02:22.920 --> 00:02:24.440
like alarms or reminders.

38
00:02:25.480 --> 00:02:30.440
However, the notifications visibility changes when the application is actively running

39
00:02:30.440 --> 00:02:35.800
in the foreground. By default, iOS does not show visual notification banners,

40
00:02:35.800 --> 00:02:38.760
if the application is currently the active window on the screen.

41
00:02:39.400 --> 00:02:43.400
To change this, Expo uses the set notification handler function.

42
00:02:43.960 --> 00:02:46.920
This allows the app to bypass the default visual suppression.

43
00:02:47.560 --> 00:02:52.040
This JavaScript function maps directly to the will present delegate in the native iOS

44
00:02:52.040 --> 00:02:56.680
framework. This gives the JavaScript thread the ability to determine whether a banner should

45
00:02:56.680 --> 00:03:02.200
be displayed even while the user is actively using the app. These triggers and handlers allow

46
00:03:02.200 --> 00:03:07.800
the JavaScript thread to manage the notification lifecycle in real time, whether the app is active

47
00:03:07.800 --> 00:03:12.600
or in the background. Moving from local alerts to remote push notifications,

48
00:03:12.600 --> 00:03:17.160
requires transitioning from the internal device environment to an external network.

49
00:03:17.800 --> 00:03:22.680
This network layer introduces native prerequisites that must be configured within the application

50
00:03:22.680 --> 00:03:27.720
build itself. In this representation of the Xcode signing and capabilities menu,

51
00:03:27.720 --> 00:03:32.120
we can see the specific entitlements required to authorize background network connections.

52
00:03:32.680 --> 00:03:37.400
The remote notifications background mode must be enabled for the application to receive

53
00:03:37.400 --> 00:03:42.360
and process payloads while it is in active. These entitlements act as digital signatures,

54
00:03:42.680 --> 00:03:47.480
verifying the application's right to Axtex Apple's network services under a specific developer

55
00:03:47.480 --> 00:03:52.920
identity. If these entitlements are missing, the operating system will drop incoming network

56
00:03:52.920 --> 00:03:58.920
payloads at the device boundary before the app can see them. Remote notifications are only possible

57
00:03:58.920 --> 00:04:03.800
once these OS level permissions are correctly bound to the application's provisioning profile.

58
00:04:04.680 --> 00:04:09.880
Once the network boundary is open, you must choose how your back end will identify and address

59
00:04:09.880 --> 00:04:16.600
the target device. Path A is the managed routing approach. This begins with the JavaScript function

60
00:04:16.600 --> 00:04:23.480
GetExpo PushTokenAsync. Using this method, the token identifies the device to expose push service.

61
00:04:24.360 --> 00:04:30.280
Payloads are sent to expose servers, which then forward them to Apple. This is the standard method

62
00:04:30.280 --> 00:04:35.960
for applications using the managed Expo ecosystem. Path B is the direct delivery method.

63
00:04:36.760 --> 00:04:42.760
Calling GetDevicePushTokenAsync returns a raw hex string instead of an Expo specific token.

64
00:04:43.560 --> 00:04:48.920
This path bypasses expose servers entirely. Your custom backend communicates directly

65
00:04:48.920 --> 00:04:54.600
with the Apple Push notification service. Path B is required for infrastructures that already

66
00:04:54.600 --> 00:05:00.840
have a dedicated pipeline for direct APNS integration. This decision creates a distinction between

67
00:05:00.840 --> 00:05:07.560
using a managed cloud service and maintaining a self-hosted delivery pipeline. When a payload successfully

68
00:05:07.560 --> 00:05:13.960
reaches the device, it is presented to the user as a visible alert. Developers use UN notification

69
00:05:13.960 --> 00:05:19.800
category to add interactive buttons to these alerts, allowing users to take action without typing.

70
00:05:20.280 --> 00:05:25.400
These categories must be registered within the application logic before the notification arrives

71
00:05:25.400 --> 00:05:31.160
to be displayed correctly. When a user taps the notification, the operating system launches or

72
00:05:31.160 --> 00:05:37.080
resumes the application to handle the interaction. A cold start occurs if the application was not

73
00:05:37.080 --> 00:05:42.920
running in memory when the tap happened. In this case, the app must boot and then retrieve the interaction

74
00:05:42.920 --> 00:05:49.000
data. During the boot sequence, iOS holds the notification payload in the application's initial launch

75
00:05:49.800 --> 00:05:55.720
options. To access this in JavaScript, Expo provides Get Last notification response async.

76
00:05:55.720 --> 00:06:00.200
This function retrieves the cached payload that triggered the launch. This is the JavaScript

77
00:06:00.200 --> 00:06:05.640
equivalent of reading notification data from launch options in a native app delegate. By using the

78
00:06:05.640 --> 00:06:11.640
Expo API as a bridge to UN notification center, you can build native grade notification handling

79
00:06:11.640 --> 00:06:13.880
entirely within the JavaScript environment.

